Subject: Report a class template's member functions through `ParseCallbacks`

bindgen discards the member functions of a class template while parsing, in
`CompInfo::from_ty`, and so says nothing about them anywhere: they are in no
callback, and `Function::codegen` would have bailed on them a second time had
they survived. The reason is sound for code generation - the set of
instantiations is open ended, so there is no monomorphization to emit - and it
throws away everything C++ said about the members along with the code nobody
could have called.

A consumer which writes its own C++ can call them. Calling a member on a chosen
instantiation instantiates it: `a.foo()` on an `A<int>` needs no symbol bindgen
emitted, only a shim naming the member and the arguments to pass it. What such a
consumer cannot do is invent the signature, which is the one thing only bindgen
knows - it is bindgen's own type mapping, down to whichever newtype-wrapper
markers the caller asked for.

`--report-template-member-functions` keeps the members at parse time and reports
each of them from the point codegen gives up, through
`denote_template_member_function`: the name, the method kind, the constness, the
access, `= delete`/`= default`, any `[[deprecated]]`, the ref-qualifier, and the
signature. Constructors and the destructor are reported too, being member
functions like any other. A class *nested* inside a class template is reported
from there as well: its own members were never discarded, having no template
parameters of their own to be discarded over, but codegen gives up on them for
the parameters they inherit and so says nothing about them either.

One thing had to be fixed rather than reported. `Cursor::is_deleted_function`
is a heuristic - an inline function with no definition and no `= default` - and
it says yes to an inline member of a class template whose body clang deferred,
which it does by default on the MSVC target, `-fdelayed-template-parsing` being
part of MSVC compatibility: the body is not parsed, and is not even in the tokens
of the declaration, so there is no definition to find. It was never
reached for such a member before, these having been discarded while parsing.

`Cursor::is_deleted_as_written` reads the declaration instead, and
`is_deleted_function_in_any_context` uses it for a member of a class template
alone, leaving every other function classified exactly as before. It reads the
last token rather than scanning: the deleted-function body is the last thing in
a declaration which has one, so whatever the declaration contains of its own - a
braced default argument, a lambda, a trailing return type, a requires-clause, an
attribute - comes before it, and nothing else a declaration can end with is the
word `delete`.

It answers `None` where the declaration cannot be read that way, which is where
a macro wrote the end of it: the tokens are then the macro's use rather than its
replacement, and may not even be the whole declaration - `void f() DELETED;`
tokenizes as one identifier, `void f() WRAP(DELETED);` as nothing past `void`,
and a declaration whose first token comes from a macro defined in another file
as nothing at all. Two things are asked for that: whether the tokens reach the
end of the declaration, and whether the last of them was written by a macro,
which the detailed preprocessing record answers. The heuristic's answer stands
for a declaration which fails either, erring towards refusing a member rather
than towards a call to a function C++ deleted.

The ref-qualifier is the one fact bindgen recorded nowhere. For a member it
generates, the qualifier survives in the mangled name the `#[link_name]`
carries, and there is no mangled name here; a class may declare both `void f()
&` and `void f() &&`, which differ in nothing else reported here, so a consumer
calling one of them by name would silently call whichever the lvalue it has
selects. `Type::cxx_ref_qualifier` asks libclang, and the answer joins the other
C++ facts on `AutocxxFuncInfo`.

The report is subject to the same per-function exclusions as codegen: a
blocklisted member is not reported, nor is one whose kind `--generate` leaves
out.

Opt-in rather than unconditional because keeping the members is not free: they
and their signatures are IR again, which costs parse time and memory on any
header which uses the standard library, where a great many class templates have
a great many members. With the option off nothing changes at all - the report is
behind the same option, so a nested class, whose members were never discarded, is
not reported either.

With it on, nothing is *generated* for them either. The existing
`all_template_params` check in `CompInfo::codegen` is what stops that, and is
where the report is made instead; and `CompInfo::trace` stops short of a
member of a class which has template parameters in scope, so the types in its
signature are not allowlisted by it. That last one is not tidiness. Tracing them
allowlists whatever a member's signature names, and for a header which includes
`<string>` that is the standard library's internals - `_CharT` and friends, whose
generated Rust does not compile. A signature naming a type nothing else asked for
is therefore reported with a name the consumer cannot resolve, which is the
consumer's to refuse; that is the trade, and it is the right way round.

The signature is reported only for a member whose signature names none of the
template parameters in scope, because a rendering which named one would be Rust
nobody outside the template can write. Which members those are is a property of
their signatures rather than of the class, so a template's members are usually a
mixture, and `names_template_parameter` decides it structurally: a parameter is
named by the parameter itself, by a type declared inside a template - bindgen
renders one with the template's parameters in scope - and by anything built out
of either. A template instantiation's arguments count and the parameters its
definition declares do not, so `A<int>` names nothing even though `A` has a
parameter - but the parameters that definition is declared *inside* do, an
instantiation of `A<T>::B<U>` supplying only `U`. The receiver is
left out of the report altogether: for a non-static member it is a pointer to
the class template, which names every parameter the template has, and a consumer
supplies its own receiver anyway - `is_const` is what tells it how to take one.

